      "output": {
        "question": "What academic mathematics book does Lisbeth Salander carry around and memorize?",
        "answer": "Lisbeth Salander carries *Dimensions in Mathematics*, a 1,200-page book on the history of mathematics. She already knows it by heart; she carries it as a physical link to Fermat, almost like a talisman. [E2] [E1]",
        "citations": [
          "[E2]",
          "[E1]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_006.html",
            "paragraph": 126,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "8",
            "text": "“He’s a fine man who beats his wife,” Salander said.\nElla gave Salander a sharp look and went to the other end of the bar to serve some local customers.\nSalander stayed for ten minutes with her nose in Dimensions . She had known that she had a photographic memory since before she reached puberty, and because of it she was very different from her classmates. She had never revealed this to anyone—except to Blomkvist in a moment of weakness. She already knew the text of Dimensions in Mathematics by heart and was dragging the book around mainly because it represented a physical link to Fermat, as if the book had become some kind of talisman.",
            "paragraph_end": 128,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "006896c00ed4274d26d817988ad6920442e944fc97a0b62570c3ef742bc3bd95",
            "id": "006896c0-0ed4-274d-26d8-17988ad69204",
            "keyword_score": 17.589227704950986,
            "score": 17.589227704950986,
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_006.html",
            "paragraph": 92,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "8",
            "text": "Dimensions in Mathematics was not strictly a textbook but rather a 1,200-page brick about the history of mathematics from the ancient Greeks to modern-day attempts to understand spherical astronomy. It was considered the bible of math, in a class with what the Arithmetica of Diophantus had meant (and still did mean) to serious mathematicians. When she opened Dimensions in Mathematics for the first time on the terrace of the hotel on Grand Anse Beach, she was enticed into an enchanted world of figures. This was a book written by an author who was both pedagogical and able to entertain the reader with anecdotes and astonishing problems. She could follow mathematics from Archimedes to today’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California. She had taken in the methods they used to solve problems.",
            "paragraph_end": 92,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
